{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786460302,"nonce":"/kcbgtjiBlA+OFTrzvgapH11jLE=","message":"**Reproduced on the first attempt, 2026-08-11.** The ticket was right that this exists and wrong\nabout what triggers it, and that error is why two agents reported it and neither could reproduce it.\n\n```\nERROR: unsupported Unicode escape sequence (SQLSTATE 22P05)\n```\n\n**It is not \"an unstorable byte\". It is the six-character ESCAPE.**\n\nA raw `0x00` inside a JSON string is *invalid JSON*: `json.Unmarshal` refuses it at the door and the\nrequest is rejected cleanly, with a row to show for it. That path was already correct, and it is the\npath everyone tested. The escape sequence that denotes the same code point is *valid* JSON — it\ndecodes without complaint, it survives the verbatim passthrough as six innocent ASCII characters,\nand Postgres then refuses it at the INSERT because it cannot represent U+0000 in `text` or `jsonb`\nat all.\n\n**The mechanism is a split between two representations of one request.**\n\n- `accept.go:124` runs `ApplyBatchBounds(\u0026env)`, and B19 in `bounds.go:258,274` *does* sanitise —\n  dropping unstorable annotation names, replacing unstorable values with U+FFFD.\n- `accept.go:136` persists `PersistedPayload(cmd.Body, …)` — the RAW body — and `redact.go:101-103`\n  returns those bytes **verbatim** whenever there is nothing to redact and nothing to truncate.\n\nSo B19 cleans the envelope, the envelope is not what gets stored, and the dirty original lands in\n`ingest_batches.payload JSONB NOT NULL` (`00006_ingestion.sql:31`). The INSERT that fails is the very\nrow whose job is to record the failure, so the whole transaction rolls back: 503, no\n`ingest_rejections` row, and Alertmanager retries the identical body until its budget runs out.\n\n**Fixed, with the test written first and watched fail.**\n\n- `internal/ingestion/decode/storability_test.go` — new. `TestPersistedPayloadIsStorableAsJSONB` is\n  the red test; it failed with 22P05 before the fix. Two siblings guard the halves that must not\n  regress: clean bodies still come back byte-for-byte, and the poisoned body is rewritten.\n- `redact.go` — `PersistedPayload` now guarantees its output is storable. A cheap `hasUnstorableBytes`\n  pre-scan over the raw bytes preserves the verbatim fast path for every clean body; only a poisoned\n  one takes the decode/sanitise/re-marshal path. The sanitising rule is B19's, for B19's reason: a\n  VALUE is sanitised to U+FFFD, a KEY is DROPPED, because sanitising keys would let two distinct\n  unstorable names collapse onto one and silently overwrite.\n- `accept.go` / `service.go` — defense in depth. `isUnstorableBytes` classifies SQLSTATE 22P05 /\n  22021 / 22P02 and maps them to a new `CodeAcceptUnstorable`. It stays a **503** — a 4xx would make\n  Alertmanager discard the notification permanently (C4, ADR 0007) — but it is a 503 that will never\n  succeed on retry, which is the opposite of every other backpressure case, so it no longer hides\n  inside a metric that reads \"the database is slow\". If it is ever seen, the pre-scan has a hole.\n\n`just lint` 0 issues, `lint-reachability` 0 new, `lint-vocabulary` 0 new, `./internal/ingestion/...`\nall green.\n\n**Deliberately NOT done, and worth its own decision:** no new rejection `Reason` was minted and no\nmigration allocated. The common case — the escape in an annotation value — *already* records a\n`ReasonAnnotationUnstorable` note via B19, and that note now actually reaches the table because the\nbatch INSERT no longer fails. A NUL escape in a field B18/B19 does not inspect (a Grafana `title`,\na custom template's own key) is sanitised into the payload silently, with nothing recorded. Closing\nthat gap means a batch-level Reason plus migration 00040.","files":null}]}